WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.580 --> 00:00:04.830
Ever since we heard that unmistakable laughter
at the end of the trailer for Star Wars Episode

00:00:04.830 --> 00:00:06.520
IX: The Rise of Skywalker...

00:00:07.080 --> 00:00:09.860
“Ha.”

00:00:11.180 --> 00:00:15.360
… we've been stunned that Emperor Palpatine
is returning for the conclusion of the Skywalker

00:00:15.360 --> 00:00:15.860
Saga.

00:00:16.020 --> 00:00:19.280
More than a few fans, however, are less stunned
than peeved.

00:00:19.820 --> 00:00:24.320
They want to know why on Earth director J.J.
Abrams and co-writer Chris Terrio would bring

00:00:24.320 --> 00:00:28.240
back a Big Bad who has supposedly been dead
for more than 30 years.

00:00:28.700 --> 00:00:32.380
Could they simply not think of any other way
to raise the stakes of the sequel's story?

00:00:32.760 --> 00:00:36.640
What kind of narrative gymnastics will Rise
of Skywalker have to engage in to make this

00:00:36.640 --> 00:00:38.400
plot development make sense?

00:00:38.620 --> 00:00:40.760
How, exactly, can this even happen?

00:00:41.260 --> 00:00:45.120
Palpatine is a central figure in both the
original and prequel trilogies.

00:00:45.360 --> 00:00:49.750
He starts as a Sith Lord in disguise serving
as a senator in the Galactic Republic, where

00:00:49.750 --> 00:00:51.580
he engineers the rise of the Empire.

00:00:51.980 --> 00:00:55.940
He successfully turns Anakin Skywalker to
the Dark Side of the Force and nearly achieves

00:00:55.940 --> 00:00:57.680
complete dominance over the galaxy.

00:00:58.140 --> 00:01:02.620
It's ultimately his rage at his inability
to turn Anakin's son Luke to the Dark Side

00:01:02.620 --> 00:01:03.780
that's his undoing.

00:01:04.180 --> 00:01:08.360
At the conclusion of Star Wars Episode VI:
Return of the Jedi, Palpatine attempts to

00:01:08.360 --> 00:01:12.300
manipulate Luke into killing his father, Anakin,
a.k.a. Darth Vader.

00:01:12.480 --> 00:01:13.760
But the plan fails.

00:01:14.140 --> 00:01:18.260
When Luke refuses to fight, Palpatine attempts
to simply kill the young Jedi, assaulting

00:01:18.260 --> 00:01:20.600
him with Force lightning as Vader watches.

00:01:20.820 --> 00:01:23.700
But Vader turns away from the Dark Side and
toward redemption.

00:01:23.980 --> 00:01:27.980
He seizes the Emperor and tosses him down
a seemingly bottomless shaft, mortally wounding

00:01:27.980 --> 00:01:29.340
himself in the process.

00:01:29.500 --> 00:01:32.720
He seemingly kills Palpatine and brings balance
to the Force.

00:01:33.040 --> 00:01:37.640
The sequel trilogy picks up 30 years after
these events, as the remnants of the Empire

00:01:37.640 --> 00:01:41.840
are reforming into the nefarious First Order
under the guidance of the Supreme Leader Snoke

00:01:41.840 --> 00:01:43.540
and his protege, Kylo Ren.

00:01:43.820 --> 00:01:49.040
Ren is Ben Skywalker, son of Han Solo and
General Leia, and a former apprentice of Luke's.

00:01:49.480 --> 00:01:54.450
Star Wars Episode VII: The Force Awakens clearly
seems to set up Snoke as the Big Bad of the

00:01:54.450 --> 00:01:59.590
sequel trilogy, but midway through Star Wars
Episode VIII: The Last Jedi, Kylo Ren kills

00:01:59.590 --> 00:02:02.000
Snoke, leaving the trilogy without a top bad
guy.

00:02:02.520 --> 00:02:04.040
"It's time to let old things die."

00:02:05.120 --> 00:02:09.460
Snoke's death was puzzling for many fans,
and was a major factor in the backlash that

00:02:09.460 --> 00:02:11.980
The Last Jedi endured from some Star Wars
fans.

00:02:12.520 --> 00:02:16.920
The tease of Palpatine's return to the franchise
stoked the ire of these fans even further,

00:02:17.050 --> 00:02:21.680
as the move is easy to interpret as an attempt
by Abrams and company to cash in on nostalgia

00:02:21.680 --> 00:02:22.980
for the original series.

00:02:23.300 --> 00:02:27.300
After all, Palpatine has not been featured
in the sequel series up to this point, and

00:02:27.300 --> 00:02:29.720
resurrecting him could be seen as lazy writing.

00:02:30.200 --> 00:02:35.060
His return raises so many questions: does
it lessen Darth Vader's heroic sacrifice?

00:02:35.520 --> 00:02:39.440
Are we supposed to now buy that Palpatine
has been pulling strings for all of the intervening

00:02:39.440 --> 00:02:42.900
years between the original and sequel trilogies,
and nobody has caught on?

00:02:43.200 --> 00:02:47.060
Did he actually die and return somehow, or
did he actually survive getting the heave-ho

00:02:47.060 --> 00:02:48.360
down a bottomless shaft?

00:02:49.240 --> 00:02:53.010
The degree to which one is willing to go along
with this plot wrinkle is going to depend

00:02:53.010 --> 00:02:54.780
largely on trust in Abrams.

00:02:55.240 --> 00:02:58.940
In a recent interview with Empire magazine,
the director made it known that he's aware

00:02:58.950 --> 00:03:02.880
that some fans are confused, and he attempted
to reassure those fans that he knows what

00:03:02.880 --> 00:03:03.380
he's doing.

00:03:03.760 --> 00:03:04.260
He said:

00:03:04.620 --> 00:03:08.840
"Some people feel like we shouldn't revisit
the idea of Palpatine, and I completely understand

00:03:08.840 --> 00:03:09.340
that.

00:03:09.500 --> 00:03:13.340
But if you're looking at these nine films
as one story, I don't know many books where

00:03:13.340 --> 00:03:16.720
the last few chapters have nothing to do with
those that have come before.

00:03:16.720 --> 00:03:20.540
If you look at the first eight films, all
the set-ups of what we're doing in [Rise of

00:03:20.540 --> 00:03:22.280
Skywalker] are there in plain view."

00:03:22.620 --> 00:03:27.319
One thing we know is that Palpatine's appearance
will be no mere cameo; he's going to be instrumental

00:03:27.319 --> 00:03:31.810
to the events of Episode IX, and if a recent
leak detailing his involvement in the narrative

00:03:31.810 --> 00:03:36.150
turns out to be accurate, his return could
actually end up making a great deal more sense

00:03:36.150 --> 00:03:37.720
than it might initially seem to.

00:03:38.140 --> 00:03:40.100
“Ha,”

00:03:41.240 --> 00:03:45.540
We'll find out the reasoning behind Palpatine's
return and surely find out how fans feel about

00:03:45.540 --> 00:03:50.280
it when Star Wars Episode IX: The Rise of
Skywalker hits the big screen December 20.

